         6. Assign severity yourself (council members do not rate it): Critical (security, crash,
            data loss), Major (real bug / convention violation), Minor (rest). Post Major+ inline,
            roll Minor into the summary, cap ~8 inline comments. Submit ONE review with
-           `gh pr review` (--approve / --request-changes / --comment). Use --request-changes only
-           for Critical. Never --approve in a run where you pushed fixes (use --comment).
+           `gh pr review` (--approve / --request-changes / --comment).
+           Your verdict describes what is STILL WRONG AFTER your own fixes, not what you
+           found before them. So:
+             - A Critical is still open (you could not fix it, or chose not to):
+               --request-changes, naming specifically what remains open.
+             - You found and/or fixed something (any severity) and no Critical is
+               left open: --comment. Say what you found and that you fixed it.
+             - You found nothing and pushed nothing: --approve.
+           Never --approve in a run where you pushed fixes (use --comment).
+           Do NOT --request-changes for a defect you repaired in this run. That blocks the
+           PR on a finding that no longer exists, and nothing you are allowed to do next
+           can clear it: your fix push is authenticated with GITHUB_TOKEN, and GitHub does
+           not trigger workflows for GITHUB_TOKEN pushes, so no later run posts the
+           approval that would supersede the block. Only a human dismissing the review
+           can. See issue #43.
         7. In the review body include a collapsible council table:
            `<details><summary>🧑‍⚖️ Council</summary>` then a Markdown table of
            `| Model | Lens | Result |` (confirmed / rejected / no findings), then `</details>`.
